<ol class="doll-sources">
  <li id="source-1">
    <a href="https://catalog.afi.com/Film/3996-FREAKS">American Film Institute, <cite>Freaks</cite></a>.
    AFI records Harry Earles as Hans, Daisy Earles as Frieda, the December 1931 completion, February 20,
    1932 release, the Tod Robbins story “Spurs,” the warning-based campaign, and Dwain Esper’s later
    reissue history.
  </li>
  <li id="source-2">
    <a href="https://www.lpaonline.org/faq-">Little People of America, “Frequently Asked Questions”</a>.
    LPA describes the range of dwarfism diagnoses, advises using names when possible, and explains why
    the period exhibition term is offensive to most people of short stature.
  </li>
  <li id="source-3">
    <a href="https://catalog.afi.com/Film/12951-THE-UNHOLYTHREE">AFI, <cite>The Unholy Three</cite> (1925)</a>
    and <a href="https://catalog.afi.com/Film/12952-THE-UNHOLY-THREE">AFI, <cite>The Unholy Three</cite> (1930)</a>.
    The records establish Harry’s Tweedledee role in both versions.
  </li>
  <li id="source-4">
    <a href="https://memphismagazine.com/ask-vance/the-famous-doll-family-s-memphis-parents/">
      Vance Lauderdale, “The Famous Doll Family’s Memphis ‘Parents,’” <cite>Memphis Magazine</cite>
    </a>.
    The column reproduces a 1936 <cite>Memphis Press-Scimitar</cite> account of the family’s Ringling tour,
    letters, postcard sales, Pomeranians, and Sarasota bungalow.
  </li>
  <li id="source-5">
    <a href="https://digital.library.illinoisstate.edu/digital/collection/p15990coll5/id/11578/">
      Ringling Bros. and Barnum &amp; Bailey route book for 1936
    </a>,
    Milner Library, Illinois State University.
  </li>
  <li id="source-6">
    <cite>The Billboard</cite>, April 24, 1937, Ringling sideshow roster,
    <a href="https://www.worldradiohistory.com/Archive-All-Music/Billboard/30s/1937/Billboard-1937-04-24.pdf">
      World Radio History scan
    </a>.
  </li>
  <li id="source-7">
    <a href="https://www.floridamemory.com/items/show/245742">Florida Memory, JJS0709</a>.
    The catalog dates the photograph to March 29, 1948, and notes the Doll Family near the train.
  </li>
  <li id="source-8">
    <a href="https://www.floridamemory.com/items/show/254650">Florida Memory, JJS2305</a> and
    <a href="https://www.floridamemory.com/share/order-info/faq.php">Florida Memory rights FAQ</a>.
    The archive identifies a Doll Family member and Joseph Janney Steinmetz but contains conflicting
    date fields. Florida Memory offers its images under Public Domain Mark 1.0.
  </li>
  <li id="source-9">
    <cite>The Billboard</cite>, January 28, 1939, Doll Family item following work on
    <cite>The Wizard of Oz</cite>,
    <a href="https://www.worldradiohistory.com/Archive-All-Music/Billboard/30s/1939/Billboard-1939-01-28.pdf">
      World Radio History scan
    </a>.
  </li>
  <li id="source-10">
    <a href="https://americanhistory.si.edu/collections/object/nmah_1888267">
      National Museum of American History, “The Doll Family,” object nmah_1888267
    </a>.
    The record identifies Gracie Doll, Tiny Doll, Harry Earles, and Daisy Earles and connects them to
    the Munchkin cast.
  </li>
  <li id="source-11">
    <a href="https://catalog.afi.com/Film/7892-THE-WIZARD-OF-OZ">
      AFI, <cite>The Wizard of Oz</cite>
    </a>.
    AFI records the collective onscreen credit, the larger recruitment beyond Leo Singer’s troupe,
    and the modern-source identification of Harry in the Lollipop Guild.
  </li>
  <li id="source-12">
    <a href="https://www.legacy.com/us/obituaries/heraldtribune/name/elly-schneider-obituary?id=50413617">
      Elly Annie Schneider obituary, <cite>Sarasota Herald-Tribune</cite>, September 10, 2004
    </a>.
  </li>
  <li id="source-13">
    <a href="https://www.floridamemory.com/items/show/268095">Florida Memory, DGM0254</a>.
    The May 1960 record states that the Sarasota quarters remained through 1959 and that Ringling moved
    the winter base to Venice in 1960.
  </li>
  <li id="source-14">
    <a href="https://www.airbnb.com/rooms/602154857519991855">
      Airbnb, “Historic Cottage in Downtown Sarasota”
    </a>,
    accessed July 26, 2026. The listing describes a 1937 two-unit home in Laurel Park and introduces
    the Doll Family connection as rumor.
  </li>
  <li id="source-15">
    Robert Plunket,
    <a href="https://www.yourobserver.com/news/2013/aug/07/home-month-enchanted-cottage/">
      “Home of the Month: Enchanted Cottage,” <cite>Your Observer</cite>
    </a>,
    August 7, 2013.
  </li>
  <li id="source-16">
    Stephen Kelly,
    <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/film/2012/apr/06/mark-povinelli-mirror-mirror-dwarfism">
      “Mark Povinelli: <cite>Mirror Mirror</cite> reflects frustrating times for dwarf actors,”
      <cite>The Guardian</cite>
    </a>,
    April 6, 2012.
  </li>
  <li id="source-17">
    <a href="https://abilitymagazine.com/danny-woodburn/">
      “Danny Woodburn — A Happyish Guy,” <cite>ABILITY Magazine</cite>
    </a>,
    October 2016.
  </li>
  <li id="source-18">
    <a href="https://www.lpaonline.org/about-lpa_">Little People of America, “About LPA”</a>.
    LPA dates its formation to Billy Barty’s 1957 Reno gathering with twenty other people of short stature.
  </li>
  <li id="source-19">
    <a href="https://the-talks.com/interview/peter-dinklage/">
      Peter Dinklage interview, <cite>The Talks</cite>
    </a>,
    September 30, 2015. Dinklage discusses <cite>Living in Oblivion</cite>, typecasting, and his discomfort
    with being treated as a spokesman.
  </li>
  <li id="source-20">
    <a href="https://www.aru.ac.uk/graduation-and-alumni/honorary-award-holders2/warwick-davis">
      Anglia Ruskin University, Warwick Davis honorary award profile
    </a>.
    The profile records Willow Management’s purpose and its <cite>Harry Potter</cite> casting work.
  </li>
  <li id="source-21">
    <a href="https://lumiere-a.akamaihd.net/v1/documents/disney_s_snow_white_final_production_notes_and_bios_1cd49638.pdf">
      Disney’s final <cite>Snow White</cite> production notes
    </a>,
    2025. The notes name the seven performers and describe the visual-effects production.
  </li>
  <li id="source-22">
    <a href="https://abcnews.go.com/GMA/Culture/disney-vows-approach-snow-white-peter-dinklage-criticism/story?id=82487821">
      ABC News, “Disney responds to Peter Dinklage’s <cite>Snow White</cite> criticism”
    </a>,
    January 26, 2022; and
    <a href="https://apnews.com/article/snow-white-box-office-alto-knights-748a8b8a94033eb1397fc7312cb9ca06">
      Associated Press, <cite>Snow White</cite> release report
    </a>,
    March 23, 2025. These sources distinguish Dinklage’s criticism, Disney’s response, and the finished
    film’s CGI characters. They do not establish that Dinklage alone caused the final production choice.
  </li>
  <li id="source-23">
    <a href="https://podcast.chrisvanvliet.com/hornswoggle-is-hilarious-little-peoples-court-dx-vinces-son-wwe-legends-deal-the-rock/">
      Dylan Postl interview, <cite>Insight with Chris Van Vliet</cite>
    </a>,
    accessed July 2026. The article uses Postl’s employment argument while leaving his personal attacks
    out of the thesis.
  </li>
</ol>

